PASTE(1)                       P32W Unix                        PASTE(1)

NAME
       paste - merge lines of files

SYNOPSIS
       paste [-s] [-d list] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       paste joins the first lines of the files into one line,
       separated by tabs, then their second lines, and so on, until
       every file has ended; a file that has ended gives empty
       parts. A file named - (or no file) is the standard input; if
       - is named more than once, each takes the next line of it in
       turn. Lines may be of any length.

OPTIONS
       -d list
              separate the parts by the characters of list in turn,
              starting again for each output line, instead of tabs.
              In list, \n is a newline, \t a tab, \\ a backslash and
              \0 nothing at all (an empty list is the same as \0).
       -s     serial: join all the lines of each file, in turn, into
              one output line (an empty file gives an empty line)

EXAMPLES
       paste names ages              side by side
       paste -s -d+ numbers          one line: 1+2+3
       ls | paste - - -              three columns
       paste -d '\n' a b             lines of a and b alternately

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error, or if a file could not be opened (with
       -s paste says why and goes on with the others; without it,
       nothing is written).

SEE ALSO
       cut(1), join(1)

BUGS
       A process may have only 16 files open (with the standard
       input, output and error), so without -s at most 13 named
       files can be pasted.
